{"abstract":"Effects of embodying virtual avatars are routinely validated experimentally by comparing synchronous and asynchronous movements between virtual and real bodies. This experimental paradigm, however, lacks justification, validation, and standardization. Asynchrony is implemented in numerous ways, such as through delayed, dislocated, or prerecorded movements, and these may impact embodiment and user experience distinctively. An online study (N = 202) revealed that variations of asynchrony cause disparate responses to embodiment and user experience, with prerecorded movements distorting embodiment the most. A think-aloud study (N = 16) revealed that asynchronous conditions lead to peculiar and oftentimes negative experiences. Furthermore, asynchronous conditions in some cases maintain, rather than break the body ownership illusion, as participants imitate the virtual body. Our results show that asynchrony in experiments on embodiment entails profound validity issues and should therefore be used with caution.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Iarygina, Olga","Hornbæk, Kasper","Mottelson, Aske"],"cuid":["S02-082.69-813959"],"event":{"end":"2025-05-01","place":"Yokohama, jp","start":"2025-04-26","title":"2025 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems, CHI 2025","year":"2025"},"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Body ownership illusions","Confounds","Embodiment","Experiments","Validity","Virtual reality"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"does random movements mean random results why asynchrony in experiments on body ownership does not work as intended","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3706598.3713506","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-082.69-813959","identifier":[{"id":"105005721021","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-5076f290-7230-4304-a933-247cf1f20c86","ku-b03f4b2b-07ca-4f91-a556-63dedf574db1"],"keyword":["body ownership illusions","confounds","embodiment","experiments","validity","virtual reality"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-783EF4BA6B0F4F6723A9560E33EB844C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3706598.3713506","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/453747361/Does_Random_Movements.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Human-Centred Computing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Olga","key":"iaryginapau","last":"Iarygina","locid":"977ec015-b047-44f1-8aa9-bda7d606c977","name":"Iarygina, Olga","orcid":"0000-0002-2419-9638","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Kasper","key":"hornbækpau","last":"Hornbæk","locid":"148d1e88-e306-46e1-9218-f420470b1ce1","name":"Hornbæk, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0001-8742-1198","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Aske","key":"mottelsonpau","last":"Mottelson","locid":"410df04c-bc1d-4cd3-9726-c35469ddab8c","name":"Mottelson, Aske","orcid":"0000-0003-1827-8513","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3706598.3713506","eisbn":["9798400713941"],"publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"CHI 2025 - Proceedings of the 2025 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105005721021"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-5076f290-7230-4304-a933-247cf1f20c86","ku-b03f4b2b-07ca-4f91-a556-63dedf574db1"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Does Random Movements mean Random Results? Why Asynchrony in Experiments on Body Ownership does not Work as Intended","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-082.69-813959","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Demand characteristics refer to cues that can inform participants in experiments about the hypothesis and influence their behavior. They lead researchers to erroneously infer non-existing effects, undermining the experimental integrity of empirical studies. Despite a widespread acknowledgment of their confounding influence in experimental psychology, experiments involving humans and computers to a lesser extent consider effects of demand characteristics, as computerized protocols are thought to be immune to some experimenter biases. Furthermore, demand characteristics are considered to mainly effect subjective measures. As a result, demand characteristics often remain uncontrolled in studies involving computers, and in particular for objective measures such as performance. In this paper, we present two experiments that underline the importance of demand characteristics in human–computer interaction experiments. In a text-entry study, we made participants believe they were evaluating a research-based keyboard. This belief led to increased performance and self-reported user experience. In a second study, we conducted a thought experiment on the illusion of body ownership in virtual reality, where the experimental design indicated the study hypothesis. We found hypothesis-compliant responses from participants, even when they did not experience the illusion. We conclude that demand characteristics pose a significant challenge to the interpretation and validity of human–computer experiments, even when they are fully automated. We discuss the implications and offer guidelines to mitigate effects of demand characteristics.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Iarygina, Olga","Hornbæk, Kasper","Mottelson, Aske"],"cuid":["S02-095.74-785373"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Bias","Confounds","Demand characteristics","Experiments","Human Computer Interaction","Validity"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"demand characteristics in human computer experiments","src":["itu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhcs.2024.103379","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-095.74-785373","identifier":[{"id":"85205678895","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-fddae596-5117-4ea3-b737-b1382bad78ca","ku-744d9c94-711a-4a38-a2a6-068620251e0b"],"keyword":["Bias","Confounds","Demand characteristics","Experiments","Human–computer interaction","Validity"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-1A1D63F4DB8BDABA60C1C53A8353117E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1071581924001629/pdfft?md5=4694005704dd14bd4ab5dca531896862&pid=1-s2.0-S1071581924001629-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/411263560/Demand_characteristics_in_human_computer_experiments.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhcs.2024.103379","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Human-Centred Computing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Olga","key":"iaryginapau","last":"Iarygina","locid":"977ec015-b047-44f1-8aa9-bda7d606c977","name":"Iarygina, Olga","orcid":"0000-0002-2419-9638","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Kasper","key":"hornbækpau","last":"Hornbæk","locid":"148d1e88-e306-46e1-9218-f420470b1ce1","name":"Hornbæk, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0001-8742-1198","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Aske","key":"mottelsonpau","last":"Mottelson","locid":"410df04c-bc1d-4cd3-9726-c35469ddab8c","name":"Mottelson, Aske","orcid":"0000-0003-1827-8513","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.ijhcs.2024.103379","issn":["10715819","10959300"],"issn_org":["10715819"],"journal":"International Journal of Human-Computer Studies","paperid":"103379","status":"p","title":"International Journal of Human-Computer Studies","title_org":"International Journal of Human Computer Studies","volume":"193"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85205678895"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-fddae596-5117-4ea3-b737-b1382bad78ca","ku-744d9c94-711a-4a38-a2a6-068620251e0b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Demand characteristics in human–computer experiments","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-095.74-785373","year":2025,"yearsub":2025}
{"abstract":"Text entry is a notably standardized research field in human-computer interaction, with established benchmarks and methodologies enabling rigorous comparisons across studies. Nevertheless, meta research in text-entry is scarce. This meta-analysis summarizes findings and effects from text entry experiments published from 1990 to 2024. Our records show that most text-entry experiments feature a baseline and an experimental UI, and that they mostly show that the experimental UI is superior (g = 1.68), with regards to text entry speed. We found that earlier text entry research focused mostly on the development of novel techniques, whereas recent text entry research, to a larger extent, adapts existing input methods to new devices, such as smartwatches or virtual reality headsets. We also find that text entry research often lacks statistical power (M = 0.66, SD = 0.37), relies on small sample sizes (Mdn = 12), and is mostly conducted with within-subjects designs (84%). Subjective evaluations of text entry systems beyond objective performance are rare. Our analysis found evidence for systematic publication bias in text entry research, as indicated by funnel plot asymmetry and a significant weight-function model adjustment. This underlines the research field’s competitive culture of publishing research only if entry speed is beaten in comparison to some baseline.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Iarygina, Olga","Mottelson, Aske"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-818248"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Keyboards","Meta analysis","Text entry"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a systematic review and meta analysis of text entry studies","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105005728890","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-818248","identifier":[{"id":"105005728890","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-95001c8e-b9e9-44c4-b654-ca8bf98db9e2"],"keyword":["Keyboards","Meta-analysis","Text entry"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-2FBB6BCF6EEF3C82BCD501A7D853A5FD"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Olga","key":"iaryginapau","last":"Iarygina","locid":"977ec015-b047-44f1-8aa9-bda7d606c977","name":"Iarygina, Olga","orcid":"0000-0002-2419-9638","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Aske","key":"mottelsonpau","last":"Mottelson","locid":"410df04c-bc1d-4cd3-9726-c35469ddab8c","name":"Mottelson, Aske","orcid":"0000-0003-1827-8513","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Naomi","key":"yamashitapedh","last":"Yamashita","name":"Yamashita, Naomi","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Vanessa","key":"everspedh","last":"Evers","name":"Evers, Vanessa","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Koji","key":"yatanipedh","last":"Yatani","name":"Yatani, Koji","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Xianghua (Sharon)","key":"dingpedh","last":"Ding","name":"Ding, Xianghua (Sharon)","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3706599.3720117","eisbn":["9798400713958"],"pages":"1-14","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"CHI EA '25: Proceedings of the Extended Abstracts of the CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105005728890"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-95001c8e-b9e9-44c4-b654-ca8bf98db9e2"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis of Text Entry Studies","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-818248","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"In the 1979 book “The Meaning of Things” Csikszentmihalyi and Rochberg-Halton studied people's perception of the significance of things in the home. They emphasized how things influence the self, and vice versa. We propose that their method and analytical framework can help to understand the analogous question for smartphones: Why are some apps special to users? Using the framework, we conduct and analyze 60 interviews with people aged 21 to 41; with participants' consent, we made the anonymized transcripts publicly available. The analysis of the interviews shows that participants find apps special because they are convenient, support personal goals and social communication, help them remember, and serve emotional functions. Participants report that their identity is intertwined with certain apps, even if they are annoying or cause dependency. Importantly, we also find that participants actively regulate their use of apps through their organization and particular use strategies.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Hornbæk, Kasper","Lyngs, Ulrik","Iarygina, Olga","Skov, Mikael B."],"cuid":["S02-100.00-793039"],"event":{"end":"2024-05-16","place":"Hybrid, Honolulu, us","start":"2024-05-11","title":"2024 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Sytems, CHI 2024","year":"2024"},"filters":{"added":"2024-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Oxford"],"keyword":["Meaning","Smartphones","User experience"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"you can find a part of my life in every single app an interview study of what makes smartphone applications special to their users","src":["aau","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-ND 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3613904.3642820","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-793039","identifier":[{"id":"85194883208","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-d174fc45-f88e-48d6-a4f0-51d9cc46ba20","ku-e5742d29-5144-4e6a-8da6-a051b4fbc7ea"],"keyword":["Smartphones","meaning","user experience"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-DD05728C8675FDAD2C105B8B65C24AA0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-ND 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/765049769/s.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-ND 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3613904.3642820","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/394530267/_You_Can_Find_a_Part_of_my_Life_in_Every_Single_App_.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Human-Centred Computing"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of oxford","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Oxford","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, AI for the People","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Dekanat, TECH","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Architecture, Design and Media Technology, Human Robot Interaction","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Kasper","key":"hornbækpau","last":"Hornbæk","locid":"148d1e88-e306-46e1-9218-f420470b1ce1","name":"Hornbæk, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0001-8742-1198","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Ulrik","key":"lyngspau","last":"Lyngs","name":"Lyngs, Ulrik","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","first":"Olga","key":"iaryginapau","last":"Iarygina","name":"Iarygina, Olga","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mikael B.","key":"skovpau","last":"Skov","locid":"c82c4a8d-1612-4218-b5ab-35e729748eb8","name":"Skov, Mikael B.","orcid":"0000-0003-4488-5683","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3613904.3642820","eisbn":["9798400703300"],"pages":"1-16","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery (ACM)","status":"p","title":"CHI 2024 - Proceedings of the 2024 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Sytems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85194883208"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-d174fc45-f88e-48d6-a4f0-51d9cc46ba20","ku-e5742d29-5144-4e6a-8da6-a051b4fbc7ea"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"“You Can Find a Part of my Life in Every Single App” : An Interview Study of What Makes Smartphone Applications Special to Their Users","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-793039","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"In a seminal paper, Banakou et al. (2013) showed that illusory ownership of a child’s body experienced through virtual reality (VR) changes perceptions and attitudes. The illusion of having the body of a 4-year-old child led participants to overestimate object sizes and react faster when associating the self with child-like attributes in an Implicit Association Test. The paper has become a model for conducting experimental embodiment research, and has impacted the study of how bodies shape minds considerably. Despite its massive impact, the study is underpowered, does not control for demand characteristics, and uses unvalidated subjective scales. The evidence of behavioral effects caused by the embodiment is therefore uncertain. To address this, we will perform a large-scale partial replication, updated to refined methodological practices with regard to objective and subjective measures, controlling for demand characteristics, and increasing the statistical power to 95%.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Iarygina, Olga","Hornbæk, Kasper","Mottelson, Aske"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776425"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Embodiment","Implicit association test","Replication","Virtual reality"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a registered replication study on body ownership perceptions and attitudes stage 000000000001 registered report protocol","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.26780176.v1","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776425","ids":["itu-97013898-f506-45c3-afa3-980898eb3eee"],"keyword":["Embodiment","implicit association test","replication","virtual reality"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-468D2B80961121CA64B860DEF45AE540"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.26780176.v1","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://springernature.figshare.com/articles/journal_contribution/A_registered_replication_study_on_body-ownership_perceptions_and_attitudes_Stage_1_Registered_Report_Protocol_/26780176","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Human-Centered Data Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Olga","key":"iaryginapau","last":"Iarygina","locid":"977ec015-b047-44f1-8aa9-bda7d606c977","name":"Iarygina, Olga","orcid":"0000-0002-2419-9638","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper","key":"hornbækpau","last":"Hornbæk","name":"Hornbæk, Kasper","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Aske","key":"mottelsonpau","last":"Mottelson","locid":"410df04c-bc1d-4cd3-9726-c35469ddab8c","name":"Mottelson, Aske","orcid":"0000-0003-1827-8513","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Al